# Q1: How would a human perform this motion? (One sentence)
A human would bring one arm across their chest, then the other arm over it, often while subtly lowering their posture or averting their gaze to appear closed-off.
# Q2: Pose Candidates Comparison. Rank at least 2 pose configurations (torso_height, arm_position, gripper_orientation, head, left_arm, x/y/z) and state the winner and why—do NOT pick presets from gesture names alone.
1. Start with arms already crossed (`arm_position: "in"`). This is just the final state and shows no action.
2. Start with arms open to the sides (`arm_position: "out", left_arm: "mirror"`). This creates a clear initial state that contrasts with the final closed posture.
Winner: #2, because the motion of crossing the arms is the most expressive part of the gesture.
# Q3: Structure Comparison. Compare 2+ full primitive sequences (e.g., M1: pose > movement > pose vs M2: pose > path > path). State the winner and why—**prioritize movement/path beats that express the cue**, not pose-only tweaks.
M1: `pose` (arms out) -> `path` (right arm arc) -> `path` (left arm arc) -> `movement` (torso/head). This structure is sequential and clear: the arms cross, then the body settles into the stance.
M2: `pose` (arms out) -> `path` (both arms cross simultaneously) -> `movement` (torso/head). This is less natural as humans cross arms sequentially, and a simultaneous motion may look robotic or cause collisions.
Winner: M1, for its natural, sequential, and readable choreography.
# Q4: Context Reinforcement. Compare 2+ micro-timing choices (speed, repetition, hold_time, torso/head expression) that make the gesture read stronger and pick the best one.
1. Perform all movements at a constant, moderate speed. This would be functional but lack emotional expression.
2. Use a quicker speed for the arm crossing to convey a decisive, closed-off action, followed by a slower, subtle torso drop and head turn to "settle" into the defensive posture.
Winner: #2, as the change in tempo enhances the defensive and withdrawn feeling of the gesture.
